from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["jezebel"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Jezebel was", """Jezebel was a princess from Sidon, a rich port city on the coast of what is now Lebanon. Her father, Ethbaal, was king of the Phoenicians. The Phoenicians were sailors and traders, and their main god was Baal, a storm and fertility god, along with a goddess named Asherah. Around 874 BC Jezebel was married to Ahab, who became king of Israel, the northern kingdom. The marriage was a political deal between two kingdoms.

The Bible says Ahab did more evil than any king before him. One reason was that he married Jezebel, and she brought her religion with her.""", ("1 Kings", 16)),
("Why her story matters", """Israel was supposed to worship only the Lord, the God who rescued them from Egypt. Jezebel is the best-known example in the Bible of what happens when a powerful person tries to replace the worship of God. She did not just believe in another god. She used the power of the crown to push everyone else toward it.""")],
cards=[
C("A queen with a mission", "Jezebel set up Baal worship across Israel and had the Lord’s prophets killed.",
"""Ahab built a temple for Baal in the capital, Samaria, and set up an Asherah pole. Jezebel fed hundreds of Baal’s prophets at her own table: 450 prophets of Baal and 400 of Asherah.

At the same time she went after the prophets of the Lord. The Bible says she was “killing off” the Lord’s prophets. A man named Obadiah, who ran Ahab’s palace, secretly hid 100 of them in two caves and fed them bread and water. That one detail tells us how dangerous it was to follow the Lord in Israel in those years. A drought had also been sent on the land, because Israel had turned from God.

So the people of Israel were being pushed hard to choose a side, and the queen was the one pushing.""", ("1 Kings", 18)),
C("The showdown on Mount Carmel", "Elijah challenged Baal’s prophets. Fire fell from heaven for the Lord, and Jezebel swore to kill Elijah.",
"""The prophet Elijah told Ahab to gather all Israel and the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel. Each side would prepare a sacrifice. The god who answered with fire would be the true God.

The prophets of Baal shouted and danced from morning until evening. Nothing happened. Elijah joked that maybe their god was busy or asleep. Then Elijah soaked his own altar with water, prayed a short prayer, and fire fell from heaven and burned up the sacrifice, the wood, the stones and the water. The people fell on their faces and said, “The Lord, He is God!” The prophets of Baal were taken down to the valley and killed, and soon after, rain finally came.

When Ahab told Jezebel everything, she did not repent. She sent Elijah a message: by this time tomorrow he would be dead like those prophets. Elijah, who had just stood alone against 450 prophets, ran for his life into the desert. Jezebel’s anger was that frightening.""", ("1 Kings", 19)),
C("Naboth’s vineyard", "When Naboth refused to sell his family land, Jezebel had him killed with false witnesses.",
"""Next to Ahab’s palace in Jezreel was a vineyard that belonged to a man named Naboth. Ahab wanted it for a vegetable garden and offered to buy it or trade for a better one. Naboth said no. The land was his family inheritance, and under God’s law an Israelite family was not supposed to sell its land away. Ahab went home sulking and would not eat.

Jezebel took charge. She wrote letters in Ahab’s name and sealed them with his royal seal. She told the city leaders to hold a public fast, seat Naboth in a place of honor, and bring two worthless men to swear that he had cursed God and the king. They did. Naboth was taken outside the city and stoned to death. Jezebel told Ahab to go and take the vineyard.

God sent Elijah to meet Ahab there. Elijah told him that dogs would lick up his blood and that dogs would eat Jezebel by the wall of Jezreel. It was God’s answer to a murder that used the law itself as a weapon.""", ("1 Kings", 21)),
C("What happened after", "Ahab died in battle. Jezebel’s family ruled a little longer, but her sons both died, and her daughter Athaliah later took the throne of Judah.",
"""About three years later Ahab was killed in a battle at Ramoth Gilead. A stray arrow found a gap in his armor. His son Ahaziah became king and died after a fall. Another son, Joram, followed him. Jezebel stayed in the palace as the queen mother, and her influence continued.

Her daughter Athaliah married into the royal family of Judah, the southern kingdom. After her husband and son died, Athaliah tried to destroy the whole royal family of David and ruled Judah herself for six years. One baby, Joash, was hidden in the Temple and saved. So Jezebel’s harm reached beyond her own kingdom.""", ("2 Kings", 11)),
C("The end of Jezebel", "Jehu was anointed king. Jezebel painted her eyes and called to him from a window. Her own servants threw her down.",
"""God sent a prophet to anoint an army commander named Jehu as king and to end the house of Ahab. Jehu killed King Joram and rode to Jezreel.

Jezebel heard he was coming. She put on eye makeup, arranged her hair and looked down from a window. She called out, “Is it peace, you Zimri, murderer of your master?” She was comparing Jehu to an earlier man who had killed a king and lasted only seven days. Even then, she was trying to stare down her enemy.

Jehu shouted up, “Who is on my side?” Two or three palace officials looked out. “Throw her down!” he said. They did. She was trampled by horses. Later, when Jehu sent men to bury her, they found only her skull, her feet and the palms of her hands, because dogs had eaten the rest. Jehu remembered what Elijah had said, and it had come true word for word.""", ("2 Kings", 9))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Jezebel’s story is a warning about power. She had wealth, a palace and an army, and she used them to silence the truth and take what belonged to someone else. In the end none of it saved her, and God’s word came true exactly as spoken.

The Bible mentions her once more, in the book of Revelation. Jesus warns the church in Thyatira about a woman there He calls “Jezebel,” who was leading people into false worship. The name had become a symbol for anyone who pulls God’s people away from Him.

The story also gives courage. Elijah looked alone, Naboth looked powerless, and Obadiah hid 100 prophets in a cave. God saw each of them. Standing for what is right can cost something, but the Bible is clear that God notices, and He has the last word.""", ("Revelation", 2))])

LONG["goliath"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Goliath was", """Goliath was a Philistine warrior from the city of Gath. The Philistines were Israel’s main enemy for hundreds of years. They lived on the coastal plain, had advanced iron weapons, and often attacked Israel’s farmland.

The Hebrew text of the Bible says Goliath was six cubits and a span tall, which is about 9 feet 9 inches. Some very old copies of the Bible say four cubits and a span, about 6 feet 9 inches. Either way, he was far bigger than anyone around him.""", ("1 Samuel", 17)),
("Why his story matters", """Goliath’s story is one of the most famous in the Bible. It is not mainly about a boy beating a giant. It is about who Israel trusted when everyone was afraid. It also shows how God works through people nobody expected.""")],
cards=[
C("The army that was afraid", "The Philistines and Israel faced each other across the Valley of Elah, and Goliath challenged anyone to fight him.",
"""Around 1020 BC the Philistines gathered their army at Socoh in Judah. King Saul and the Israelite army camped on the opposite hill. Between them lay the Valley of Elah, a wide valley with a stream running through it.

Goliath came out of the Philistine camp every morning and every evening for forty days. He wore a bronze helmet and a coat of scale armor that weighed about 125 pounds. He had bronze armor on his legs, a javelin slung on his back, and a spear with a shaft like a weaver’s beam, tipped with an iron head weighing about 15 pounds. A shield bearer walked ahead of him.

His challenge was simple. Send one man to fight me. If he wins, we will be your slaves. If I win, you will be ours. When Saul and the Israelites heard it, they were “dismayed and terrified.” Saul was the king, and he stood a head taller than anyone else in Israel, yet he stayed in his tent. Nobody moved for forty days.""", ("1 Samuel", 17)),
C("A shepherd boy arrives", "David came with bread and cheese for his brothers, heard the challenge, and said he would fight.",
"""David was the youngest son of Jesse of Bethlehem. He was probably a teenager. He went back and forth between Saul’s court, where he played the harp, and the family sheep. His three oldest brothers were in Saul’s army, so one day Jesse sent David to the camp with grain, ten loaves and ten cheeses.

David arrived as Goliath came out for his challenge. He heard it, and he saw the soldiers run back. He asked a question nobody else had asked: “Who is this uncircumcised Philistine that he should defy the armies of the living God?” His oldest brother, Eliab, got angry and accused him of showing off. David kept asking.

Someone told Saul, and David was brought in. He told the king he had killed both a lion and a bear while watching his father’s sheep. “The Lord who rescued me from the paw of the lion and the paw of the bear will rescue me from the hand of this Philistine.” Saul, with no better option, said, “Go, and the Lord be with you.”""", ("1 Samuel", 17)),
C("Five smooth stones", "David refused Saul’s armor, took a sling and five stones, and went out in the name of the Lord.",
"""Saul tried to dress David in his own bronze helmet, coat of armor and sword. David walked around a bit and said he couldn’t wear it, because he had not tested it. He took it all off.

Instead he took his shepherd’s staff, picked five smooth stones from the stream and put them in his bag, and took his sling. A sling was a real weapon. Skilled slingers could hit a target with great speed and accuracy.

Goliath saw him coming and was insulted. “Am I a dog, that you come at me with sticks?” He cursed David by his gods and said he would feed his body to the birds. David answered, “You come against me with sword and spear and javelin, but I come against you in the name of the Lord of hosts. This day the Lord will deliver you into my hand. And all this assembly will know that the Lord saves not with sword and spear. For the battle is the Lord’s.”""", ("1 Samuel", 17)),
C("The stone and the sword", "David ran toward Goliath, struck him in the forehead with one stone, and the Philistines fled.",
"""David did not hang back. He ran toward the battle line, took a stone from his bag, slung it, and hit Goliath in the forehead. The giant fell on his face. David had no sword, so he ran up, took Goliath’s own sword out of its sheath, and used it to cut off his head.

When the Philistines saw their champion dead, they ran. The men of Israel and Judah shouted, chased them all the way to Gath and Ekron, and then returned to take the Philistine camp.

David took the head to Jerusalem, and put the armor in his own tent. Later, when he was running from Saul and needed a weapon, a priest at Nob gave him the one thing he had stored there: the sword of Goliath. He said, “There is none like it. Give it to me.”""", ("1 Samuel", 21)),
C("After the giant", "The victory made David famous, and it began the long road to his becoming king.",
"""The women of Israel came out singing and dancing: “Saul has struck down his thousands, and David his ten thousands.” The song made Saul jealous, and from that day he watched David with suspicion. It started a long chain of events: David had to run for years, but he eventually became Israel’s greatest king.

The Philistines did not disappear. Later the Bible tells of other warriors from Gath who were also of great size, and David’s men fought and defeated them. Gath itself, Goliath’s home city, would one day shelter David when he was a fugitive.""", ("2 Samuel", 21))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Almost everyone has a “Goliath”: a problem that looks too big, a fear that has been shouting at you for forty days. This story does not say the problem was small. It says David did not measure the giant against himself. He measured him against God.

It also shows that God does not need what the world calls impressive. Israel had a tall king and a trained army, and nobody would go. God used a young shepherd with a sling because he trusted the Lord.

The story points ahead to Jesus, who came from David’s town of Bethlehem and defeated sin and death, the biggest enemies of all. Courage in the Bible is not feeling brave. It is trusting that the battle belongs to the Lord.""", ("1 Samuel", 17))])

LONG["herod"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Herod the Great was", """Herod was king of Judea from 37 BC until his death in 4 BC. He was not a full Israelite by descent. His family came from Idumea, the land of Edom, south of Judea, and had been forced to adopt Judaism a century earlier. Rome made him king because he was loyal to Rome. The Roman Senate named him “King of the Jews” in 40 BC, and he then spent three years fighting to take Jerusalem.

He was one of the greatest builders of the ancient world. He also became one of its most feared rulers.""", ("Matthew", 2)),
("Why his story matters", """Herod appears in the Bible at the moment Jesus was born. He is the first king to hear that a new king of the Jews has been born, and the first to try to kill Him. His story shows what happens when a ruler is more afraid of losing power than of God.""")],
cards=[
C("The great builder", "Herod rebuilt the Temple in Jerusalem and built cities, fortresses and harbors.",
"""Herod built a whole new harbor city on the coast, called Caesarea, named for the emperor. He built the fortress-palaces of Herodium and Masada, and added to many cities. His biggest project was the Temple in Jerusalem. Around 20 BC he began to rebuild it on a much larger scale, with huge white stones, gold decoration and massive courts. It was still being worked on in Jesus’ day. John’s Gospel records someone saying it had been under construction “forty-six years.”

He did it to win favor with the Jewish people, who never fully trusted him, and with Rome. Many of his buildings could be seen for miles, and some walls still stand today, including part of the foundation around the Temple Mount.""", ("John", 2)),
C("A king afraid of losing his throne", "Herod killed his own wife, his in-laws and three of his sons because he suspected they wanted his power.",
"""Herod was famously suspicious. He had his wife Mariamne, from the old Jewish royal family, executed. He also had her brother and her grandfather killed. Later he had two of his sons by Mariamne strangled. Just five days before he died, he ordered the execution of his oldest son, Antipater, for plotting against him.

The Jewish historian Josephus, who wrote about Herod, describes a man full of fear, rage and illness in his last years. So when wise men arrived in Jerusalem and asked, “Where is the one who has been born king of the Jews?”, Herod’s reaction is not a surprise. Matthew says he was “troubled, and all Jerusalem with him.” Everyone knew what Herod did when he felt threatened.""", ("Matthew", 2)),
C("The wise men", "Herod called the priests, learned that the Messiah would be born in Bethlehem, and asked the wise men to report back.",
"""The wise men, or magi, came from the East, probably Persia or Babylon, following a star. They went straight to Jerusalem, the capital, because that is where a king would be born.

Herod gathered the chief priests and teachers of the law and asked where the Messiah was to be born. They answered from the prophet Micah: Bethlehem in Judea. Herod secretly called the magi, learned exactly when the star had appeared, and sent them to Bethlehem. “Go and search carefully for the child. As soon as you find him, report to me, so that I too may go and worship him.”

He did not want to worship. He wanted to find the child. The priests knew the Scripture and were only five or six miles away, yet none of them went to see Jesus. The magi did. They found the child with Mary, bowed down and gave gifts: gold, frankincense and myrrh. A warning came in a dream, and they went home another way.""", ("Matthew", 2)),
C("Bethlehem", "When the wise men did not return, Herod ordered all boys in Bethlehem aged two and under to be killed. Joseph took his family to Egypt.",
"""God warned Joseph in a dream that Herod was going to search for the child to kill Him. Joseph got up in the night and took Mary and Jesus to Egypt.

Herod saw that the wise men had tricked him and was furious. He sent soldiers to kill all the boys in Bethlehem and the surrounding area who were two years old and under. Based on what the wise men had told him about the star, he chose the age to be safe. The Bible does not give a number. Bethlehem was a small town, so historians think the number was small, but every child was a terrible loss. Matthew says this fulfilled a prophecy of Jeremiah about Rachel weeping for her children.

Herod’s cruelty fits what history tells us about him. It also shows that the birth of Jesus was a real threat to the powers of the world from the very beginning.""", ("Matthew", 2)),
C("How Herod died", "Herod died in 4 BC. Joseph then brought Jesus home to Nazareth in Galilee.",
"""Herod died around 4 BC. Josephus describes his final illness as painful and terrible, and says he lived his last days in fear that no one would mourn him. Before he died he ordered important men to be locked up, so there would be mourning at his death. The order was not carried out.

After his death an angel told Joseph in a dream that it was safe to return. Herod’s kingdom was divided among three sons. Archelaus took Judea and was so cruel that Joseph was afraid to settle there, so he took Jesus to Nazareth in Galilee instead. Herod Antipas, another son, ruled Galilee and later killed John the Baptist. Herod’s family kept showing up in the Gospels and in the book of Acts, still opposing Jesus and His followers.""", ("Matthew", 2))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Herod had a palace, an army and a great temple, and he died in fear. A baby in a poor family’s house in Bethlehem outlasted him. Herod’s throne is gone, and Jesus is still worshiped around the world.

The story is also a warning. The priests knew the Scriptures and ignored the news. Herod heard the same news and tried to destroy it. The wise men, who were outsiders, simply followed and worshiped. How a person reacts to Jesus shows what is really in the heart.

Finally, the story is comfort for anyone who has suffered because of a powerful person’s selfishness. God was present in Bethlehem’s sorrow, and the Bible shows that Jesus came into a world with real evil in it, to defeat it.""", ("Matthew", 2))])

LONG["haman"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Haman was", """Haman is the villain in the book of Esther. The Bible calls him “the Agagite,” which means he was probably a descendant of Agag, the king of the Amalekites. The Amalekites were an ancient enemy of Israel, and centuries earlier King Saul had been told to destroy them and had spared Agag. Now, about 474 BC, Haman’s family line was alive again in Persia.

King Xerxes, called Ahasuerus in the Bible, ruled the vast Persian Empire from India to Africa. He promoted Haman above all the other officials. Everyone at the palace gate had to bow when Haman passed.""", ("Esther", 3)),
("Why his story matters", """Haman’s plan was to destroy every Jewish man, woman and child in the empire in a single day. The book of Esther never mentions God by name, yet the story shows God’s hand in every turn. Jews still celebrate the rescue each year at the festival of Purim.""")],
cards=[
C("The man who would not bow", "Mordecai, a Jewish official, refused to bow to Haman, and Haman decided to punish all the Jews.",
"""Everyone at the king’s gate bowed to Haman. One man would not: Mordecai, a Jew whose family had been carried off from Jerusalem to Babylon generations earlier. Day after day the other officials asked why he disobeyed. Mordecai said he was a Jew. He would not give honor that belonged to God alone.

When Haman heard, he was furious. Killing one man was not enough for him. He found out who Mordecai’s people were and decided to destroy them all. He would do it legally, using the empire’s own government.

Haman cast the “pur,” which means lots, to choose a day. The lot chose the 13th day of the twelfth month, nearly a year away. Then he went to the king and said there was a certain people scattered through the empire whose laws were different and who did not obey the king’s laws. He offered 10,000 talents of silver to the royal treasury, an enormous amount, if the king would order them destroyed. Xerxes took his signet ring off and handed it to Haman. “Keep the money, and do with the people as you please.”""", ("Esther", 3)),
C("Mordecai and Esther", "Mordecai asked Queen Esther to go to the king, even though going to him uninvited could mean death.",
"""Letters were sent in every language to every province: on the 13th day of the twelfth month, kill all the Jews, young and old, and take their property. Then the king and Haman sat down to drink, and the city of Susa was in confusion.

Mordecai put on sackcloth and ashes and went into the city weeping. Esther, his cousin whom he had raised, had been made queen without telling anyone she was Jewish. Mordecai sent word that she must go to the king and plead for her people. Esther answered that anyone who went to the king without being called could be put to death unless he held out his golden scepter. She had not been called in thirty days.

Mordecai’s reply is one of the best-known lines in the Bible: “Who knows whether you have not come to the kingdom for such a time as this?” Esther told him to gather the Jews in Susa and fast for her for three days. “Then I will go to the king, though it is against the law, and if I perish, I perish.”""", ("Esther", 4)),
C("The banquet and the gallows", "Esther invited the king and Haman to dinner. Haman’s wife told him to build gallows for Mordecai.",
"""On the third day Esther put on her royal robes and stood in the inner court. The king saw her and held out the golden scepter. She did not make her request. She invited the king and Haman to a banquet. At the banquet she invited them to a second one the next day.

Haman left happy, until he passed Mordecai at the gate and Mordecai still would not stand or tremble. At home Haman bragged about his riches, his sons, and his honors, then said none of it mattered while he saw Mordecai the Jew sitting at the gate. His wife and friends told him to build a gallows 50 cubits high, about 75 feet, and ask the king in the morning to hang Mordecai on it. Haman liked the idea and built it that night.

That same night the king could not sleep. He asked for the royal records to be read to him, and the reader came to the day when Mordecai had uncovered a plot to assassinate the king. Nobody had ever rewarded him.""", ("Esther", 5)),
C("The turn", "The king asked Haman how to honor a man. Haman thought it was himself, and then had to honor Mordecai.",
"""In the morning Haman walked in to ask the king for permission to hang Mordecai. Before he could speak, the king asked him, “What should be done for the man the king delights to honor?” Haman thought, “Who would the king delight to honor more than me?” He described a grand parade: the king’s robe, the king’s horse, a royal crown, and a noble leading him through the city square shouting his praise.

“Go quickly,” the king said, “and do just as you have said for Mordecai the Jew.” Haman had to dress Mordecai, put him on the king’s horse, and lead him through the city shouting, “This is what is done for the man the king delights to honor!” Then Mordecai went back to the gate, and Haman hurried home with his head covered in shame. His wife and advisors told him, “If Mordecai is of Jewish descent, you will not overcome him, but will surely fall before him.” Before they finished speaking, the king’s men arrived to take him to Esther’s second banquet.""", ("Esther", 6)),
C("Haman’s end", "Esther told the king about the plot. Haman was hanged on the gallows he had built for Mordecai.",
"""At the second banquet the king asked Esther again what she wanted. She said, “If I have found favor with you, O king, let my life be given me, and my people. For we have been sold, I and my people, to be destroyed, to be killed and to be annihilated.” The king asked who dared to do this. Esther pointed. “A foe and enemy! This wicked Haman!”

The king stormed out into the garden. Haman, terrified, stayed to beg Esther for his life, and fell on the couch where she was reclining. The king came back and saw him. “Will he even assault the queen in my presence?” Servants covered Haman’s face. One of them said, “The gallows that Haman made for Mordecai stands at Haman’s house, 75 feet high.” The king said, “Hang him on that.” Haman was hanged on his own gallows.

Persian law could not be canceled. So the king let Mordecai write a second decree allowing the Jews to defend themselves on that day. On the 13th they defended themselves and won. Mordecai became the second most important man in the empire, and the people celebrated.""", ("Esther", 7))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Haman’s hatred started with a small thing: one man would not bow. Pride and anger, left alone, grew into a plan to destroy a whole people. The Bible shows how dangerous unchecked pride can be, and how it often destroys the person who carries it.

The story also shows that God works behind the scenes. His name is never mentioned in Esther, yet the right person was in the right place: a sleepless king, a forgotten act of loyalty, and a queen who was willing to risk her life. When it seems that God is silent, He may be arranging things we cannot yet see.

And Esther’s courage still speaks to us. “For such a time as this” is a question every believer may face: you have been put where you are for a reason, and God may ask you to step forward.""", ("Esther", 4))])

LONG["pharaoh"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Pharaoh was", """“Pharaoh” was the title of the king of Egypt, not a personal name. The Bible tells the story of two different pharaohs in the book of Exodus: the one who began to oppress the Israelites, and a later pharaoh who faced Moses. The Bible does not give their names, and scholars have long debated which kings they were. Some think the Exodus happened in the 1400s BC, and others in the 1200s BC.

Egyptians believed their pharaoh was a god in human form, the son of the sun god Ra. That is why a confrontation with Pharaoh was also a confrontation with Egypt’s gods.""", ("Exodus", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """The story of Pharaoh and Moses is the story of the rescue that Israel remembered for the rest of its history. Every year the Jewish people still celebrate Passover to remember it. It is the story of what the Lord does for people who cannot save themselves.""")],
cards=[
C("A king who forgot", "A new pharaoh “who did not know Joseph” made the Israelites slaves and ordered their baby boys killed.",
"""Centuries before, Joseph had saved Egypt from famine, and his family, the Israelites, settled in the land of Goshen. They grew into a great nation. A new pharaoh arose who did not remember Joseph. He saw the Israelites as a danger: if war came, they might join Egypt’s enemies.

So he put them to forced labor, building the store cities of Pithom and Raamses with mud bricks. They were forced to work “with harsh labor.” Even so they kept multiplying. Pharaoh then told two Hebrew midwives, Shiphrah and Puah, to kill every Hebrew baby boy at birth. They feared God and refused, telling Pharaoh that Hebrew women gave birth too quickly. When that failed, Pharaoh ordered every newborn boy thrown into the Nile.

One baby was hidden for three months, then placed in a basket on the river. Pharaoh’s own daughter found him, and he grew up in the palace. His name was Moses.""", ("Exodus", 1)),
C("“Let my people go”", "Decades later Moses returned from the desert and told the new Pharaoh: “Let my people go.”",
"""Moses fled Egypt as a young man after killing an Egyptian, and lived forty years as a shepherd in Midian. Then God spoke to him from a burning bush. The old pharaoh had died, and God sent Moses back with his brother Aaron.

They went to Pharaoh and said, “Thus says the Lord, the God of Israel: Let My people go, that they may hold a feast to Me in the wilderness.” Pharaoh answered, “Who is the Lord, that I should obey His voice? I do not know the Lord, and moreover, I will not let Israel go.” That day he made the work worse, ordering the slaves to make the same number of bricks while gathering their own straw.

The Israelite foremen blamed Moses. Moses went back to God, troubled. But God said, “Now you will see what I will do to Pharaoh.” God was about to show Egypt and Israel who He was.""", ("Exodus", 5)),
C("The ten plagues", "God sent ten plagues on Egypt, each one aimed at something Egyptians worshiped.",
"""Over the next months, ten plagues struck Egypt. The Nile turned to blood. Frogs, gnats and flies swarmed the land. The livestock died. Boils broke out on people and animals. Hail destroyed the crops. Locusts ate whatever was left. Then three days of thick darkness covered Egypt, while the Israelites in Goshen had light.

Many of these plagues hit things Egypt worshiped: the Nile, frogs, cattle, and the sun. The Bible says God was bringing “judgment on all the gods of Egypt.” After several plagues Pharaoh said he would let Israel go, then changed his mind when the plague stopped. The Bible says that Pharaoh hardened his own heart, and later that God hardened it too. Pharaoh was given chance after chance and kept choosing to refuse.

At one point his own officials told him, “Do you not yet understand that Egypt is destroyed?”""", ("Exodus", 7)),
C("The Passover night", "The tenth plague killed every firstborn in Egypt, except in the homes marked with lamb’s blood.",
"""God told Moses that one more plague would come. At midnight every firstborn son in Egypt would die, from Pharaoh’s own son to the child of the lowest prisoner.

God gave Israel a way to be spared. Each family was to kill a young lamb with no defect, put some of its blood on the doorposts, and eat the meal in a hurry, ready to leave. “When I see the blood, I will pass over you.” That night a loud cry rose across Egypt, because there was not a house without someone dead. Pharaoh summoned Moses and Aaron in the night and said, “Rise up, go out from among my people, you and the Israelites. Go, serve the Lord.”

This is why Jesus is called “the Lamb of God.” The Bible teaches that His blood covers people the way the lamb’s blood covered those homes. Jesus ate a Passover meal with His disciples on the night before He died.""", ("Exodus", 12)),
C("The sea", "Pharaoh changed his mind and chased Israel with chariots, and God opened the Red Sea.",
"""Israel left, about 600,000 men on foot plus women and children, and a large mixed crowd with them. But Pharaoh soon regretted it. “What have we done, that we have let Israel go from serving us?” He took 600 of his best chariots and the whole Egyptian army.

They caught the Israelites camped by the sea. The people panicked: the sea in front, the army behind. Moses said, “Do not be afraid. Stand firm and see the salvation of the Lord.” God told Moses to lift his staff. A strong east wind blew all night and the sea split. Israel walked through on dry ground with walls of water on both sides.

Pharaoh’s army followed. When the Israelites were safely across, Moses stretched out his hand again, and the water came back and covered the chariots, the horsemen and the whole army. Not one survived. The Bible does not say whether Pharaoh himself drowned; it says the entire army did. The people saw the Egyptians dead on the shore. Then Moses and Miriam led Israel in a song: “The Lord is my strength and my song, and He has become my salvation.”""", ("Exodus", 14))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Pharaoh is a picture of a human heart that says, “Who is the Lord, that I should obey Him?” Every plague was a new chance to say yes. Each time he refused, it became harder to turn. The Bible warns us not to harden our hearts when we hear God.

The apostle Paul writes that God raised up Pharaoh so that His power would be shown and His name proclaimed in all the earth. Even a mighty ruler could not stop God’s plan.

The story is also good news. The Israelites did nothing to earn their rescue. They were slaves with no army, and God saved them, the way the Bible says He saves us. No power on earth is too strong for Him.""", ("Romans", 9))])

LONG["pilate"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Pilate was", """Pontius Pilate was the Roman governor of Judea from about AD 26 to 36 or 37. His official title was prefect. He was appointed by the emperor Tiberius and lived mostly in the coastal city of Caesarea, traveling to Jerusalem for big festivals such as Passover to keep order. In 1961, archaeologists found a stone at Caesarea with his name and title on it. Roman writers like Tacitus also mention him.

Jewish writers of the time describe him as harsh. Philo and Josephus tell of him bringing Roman military standards with the emperor’s image into Jerusalem, which nearly caused a riot, and using Temple money to build an aqueduct, which led to protests that his soldiers put down by force. Luke mentions a time when Pilate killed some Galileans while they were offering sacrifices.""", ("Luke", 13)),
("Why his story matters", """Pilate is the man who sentenced Jesus to death. His name is in the Apostles’ Creed, which the church has said for centuries: “suffered under Pontius Pilate.” That line fixes the death of Jesus in real history, at a real time, under a real governor.""")],
cards=[
C("Jesus brought to Pilate", "The Jewish leaders arrested Jesus at night and brought Him to Pilate early in the morning.",
"""After Jesus was arrested in the garden of Gethsemane, the Jewish council tried Him during the night and decided He deserved death for claiming to be the Son of God. Under Roman rule they did not have the authority to carry out a death sentence, so at dawn they brought Jesus to Pilate. They stayed outside the palace so that they would not become ritually unclean before the Passover meal.

Because they could not charge Him with a religious crime in a Roman court, they changed the charges. They said Jesus was stirring up the people, telling them not to pay taxes to Caesar, and claiming to be a king. Pilate, who cared mostly about order and about Caesar, asked Jesus: “Are you the King of the Jews?”""", ("John", 18)),
C("The question of truth", "Jesus told Pilate His kingdom is not of this world. Pilate asked, “What is truth?”",
"""Pilate took Jesus inside. Jesus answered that His kingdom was not of this world. If it were, His followers would be fighting. “You say that I am a king. For this purpose I was born and for this purpose I have come into the world, to testify to the truth. Everyone who is of the truth listens to My voice.”

Pilate said, “What is truth?” and went back out without waiting for an answer. He told the crowd, “I find no guilt in Him.” The charge was not true, and Pilate knew it. Luke adds that when Pilate learned Jesus was from Galilee, he sent Him to Herod Antipas, who ruled there and happened to be in Jerusalem. Herod and his soldiers mocked Jesus and sent Him back.""", ("John", 18)),
C("The crowd and Barabbas", "Pilate tried to release Jesus. The crowd demanded Barabbas instead and shouted, “Crucify Him!”",
"""It was Pilate’s custom to release one prisoner at Passover. He offered the crowd a choice between Jesus and a known criminal, Barabbas, who had taken part in a riot and murder. Pilate understood that the chief priests had handed Jesus over “out of envy.” He expected the crowd to ask for Jesus.

Meanwhile his wife sent him a message: “Have nothing to do with that righteous man, for I have suffered much because of Him today in a dream.” But the chief priests persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas. “What shall I do with Jesus who is called Christ?” Pilate asked. “Let Him be crucified!” Pilate asked, “Why? What evil has He done?” They shouted all the louder.

Pilate had Jesus whipped. Roman soldiers put a purple robe on Him and a crown of thorns, and mocked Him as king. Pilate brought Him out and said, “Behold the man!” Still the crowd shouted for crucifixion.""", ("Matthew", 27)),
C("Washing his hands", "Pilate saw that a riot was starting. He washed his hands in front of the crowd and handed Jesus over.",
"""The leaders then said, “If you release this man, you are no friend of Caesar.” That was a real threat. A report to Rome that Pilate had let a man claiming to be king go free could end his career, or his life. Pilate sat on his judgment seat, the stone pavement, and said, “Behold your King!” They shouted, “Away with Him! Crucify Him!” “We have no king but Caesar,” the chief priests said.

Pilate took water and washed his hands before the crowd. “I am innocent of this man’s blood; see to it yourselves.” But the water did not make it so. He still gave the order. He knew Jesus was innocent and put Him to death anyway. Pilate also had a sign written and fixed to the cross: “Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews,” in Hebrew, Latin and Greek. When the priests asked him to change it, he said, “What I have written, I have written.”""", ("Matthew", 27)),
C("After the cross", "Pilate let Joseph of Arimathea bury Jesus, and he ordered a guard at the tomb. He was removed from office a few years later.",
"""That evening a wealthy member of the council named Joseph of Arimathea, a secret follower of Jesus, asked Pilate for the body. Pilate was surprised Jesus had died so quickly. He checked with the centurion and then gave permission.

The next day the chief priests asked Pilate to seal the tomb and post guards, since Jesus had said He would rise on the third day. Pilate said, “You have a guard of soldiers. Go, make it as secure as you can.” They sealed the stone and set the guard. On the third morning, the tomb was empty.

The Bible says nothing more about Pilate. Outside the Bible, Josephus says that around AD 36 he attacked a crowd of Samaritans gathered at Mount Gerizim. The Roman governor of Syria removed him and sent him to Rome. What happened to him after that, we do not know. Later legends give different endings, but none is reliable.""", ("Matthew", 27))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Pilate shows how a person can know what is right and still do what is wrong. He said Jesus was innocent three times. He felt the pressure of the crowd, the priests, and his own career, and he gave in. Washing his hands did not clear him.

Many of us will never face a decision as large as his, but we do face small moments when doing right costs us something. The story asks what we will do when the crowd is loud.

It also shows something larger. Pilate and the crowd thought they were in control, but Jesus told him, “You would have no authority over Me at all unless it had been given you from above.” The Bible teaches that Jesus went to the cross willingly, to take the punishment for our sins. Even the worst injustice in history became the way God saved the world.""", ("John", 19))])

LONG["caleb"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Caleb was", """Caleb, the son of Jephunneh, was a man of the tribe of Judah who lived about 1400 BC. He grew up as a slave in Egypt, saw the plagues, crossed the Red Sea, and stood at the foot of Mount Sinai when God gave the Law. By the time of this story he was about 40 years old.

He was one of the twelve men Moses chose to explore the land of Canaan, the land God had promised to Abraham’s family.""", ("Numbers", 13)),
("Why his story matters", """Caleb’s story is about a person who trusted God when almost everyone else did not. It also shows what it means to follow God “wholeheartedly,” a phrase the Bible uses about Caleb more than once.""")],
cards=[
C("Twelve spies", "Moses sent one leader from each tribe to explore Canaan for forty days.",
"""Israel had been camped for a long time in the wilderness, and now they stood on the border of the promised land. Moses sent twelve men, one from each tribe, to see what the land and the people were like. Caleb represented Judah. Joshua represented Ephraim.

For forty days they traveled through the land. They came to Hebron, where Abraham, Isaac and Jacob were buried, and to the valley of Eshcol. There they cut a single cluster of grapes so large that two men carried it on a pole between them, along with pomegranates and figs. When they returned, they showed the fruit to everyone: “It flows with milk and honey, and this is its fruit.”""", ("Numbers", 13)),
C("A different report", "Ten spies said the land could not be taken. Caleb said, “We should go up and take the land.”",
"""Then ten of the spies added: “But the people who live there are strong, and the cities are fortified and very large. We also saw descendants of Anak there.” The Anakites were famous for their size. The ten said, “We seemed like grasshoppers in our own eyes, and we looked the same to them.”

The people cried all night. Some said they should choose a new leader and go back to Egypt. Caleb tried to calm them: “We should go up and take possession of the land, for we are well able to overcome it.” Caleb and Joshua tore their clothes and said, “The land is exceedingly good. If the Lord delights in us, He will bring us into this land. Only do not rebel against the Lord. Do not fear the people of the land. The Lord is with us.”

The crowd threatened to stone them.""", ("Numbers", 14)),
C("Forty years", "God judged the people’s unbelief: they would wander forty years, and only Caleb and Joshua would enter.",
"""God was angry that after all He had done, they still would not trust Him. He said that no one from that generation over twenty years old, except Caleb and Joshua, would see the land. They would wander in the wilderness one year for every day the spies had explored: forty years. The ten spies who had spread fear died of a plague.

About Caleb, God said, “My servant Caleb, because he has a different spirit and has followed Me fully, I will bring into the land into which he went, and his descendants shall possess it.”

Caleb then spent forty years in the desert, watching all his friends die one by one. He could have given up or become bitter. The Bible does not say he did. Over those years he kept trusting the promise.""", ("Numbers", 14)),
C("“Give me this mountain”", "At age 85, Caleb asked Joshua for Hebron, the hill country where the giants lived.",
"""After Moses died, Joshua led Israel into the land. The conquest took years. Eventually, as the land was being divided among the tribes, Caleb came to Joshua at Gilgal. He said: “I was forty years old when Moses sent me to spy out the land. And now, behold, I am eighty-five years old. I am still as strong today as the day Moses sent me. As my strength was then, so my strength is now, for war and for going and coming.”

Then he made his request: “So now give me this hill country of which the Lord spoke on that day. For you heard on that day how the Anakim were there, with great fortified cities. It may be that the Lord will be with me, and I shall drive them out as the Lord said.” In other words, at 85 he asked for the hardest place of all, the very mountain full of the giants that had frightened Israel.

Joshua blessed him and gave him Hebron. Caleb drove out the three Anakite clans, and the land had rest from war.""", ("Joshua", 14)),
C("A family of faith", "Caleb’s nephew Othniel became Israel’s first judge, and his daughter Achsah asked for springs of water.",
"""Caleb offered his daughter Achsah in marriage to whoever captured the city of Kiriath Sepher. His nephew Othniel did it and married her. Othniel later became the first of Israel’s judges and rescued Israel from an invader.

When Achsah married, she asked her father for a field, and then for something more: “Give me a blessing. Since you have given me land in the Negeb, give me also springs of water.” Caleb gave her upper and lower springs. Water was life in a dry land, and she was wise to ask. Caleb’s home was marked by trust in God and boldness in asking.

Hebron, Caleb’s mountain, kept its place in the story. Centuries later it became the first capital of King David.""", ("Judges", 1))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Caleb teaches us that a majority can be wrong. Ten of the twelve spies saw the same land and the same giants as Caleb. The difference was not what they saw. It was whom they were looking at. Caleb looked at the Lord first.

His story also shows that faith lasts. It is one thing to be brave on a good day. Caleb held on to a promise for 45 years and still asked for a mountain at age 85. Following God can be a long road, and the Bible says it is worth it.

If you are older, Caleb’s story says it is never too late to take a mountain. And if you are young, it says that who you follow now will shape the rest of your life.""", ("Joshua", 14))])

LONG["shadrach"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego were", """Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego were three young Jewish men from Judah. Their Hebrew names were Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah. About 605 BC, King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon attacked Jerusalem and took some of the best young men of the royal family and nobility back to Babylon. The three were probably teenagers. They were taken with a friend named Daniel.

The Babylonians gave them new names that honored Babylonian gods. The Bible uses the Babylonian names in the story of the furnace: Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego.""", ("Daniel", 1)),
("Why their story matters", """Their story is about being faithful to God in a foreign land where the powers that be demand something else. They did not have a temple, a king or an army. They had only their decision about whom they would obey.""")],
cards=[
C("Training in Babylon", "The young men were trained for three years to serve the king, but they chose not to eat the king’s food.",
"""Babylon was the largest city in the world, with huge walls, a famous hanging garden and a great tower temple. The king wanted the best young men trained in the language and literature of the Babylonians for three years before entering his service. They were to be fed the king’s own food and wine.

Daniel and his three friends decided not to defile themselves with the king’s food, which probably included foods the law of Moses forbade, and was likely dedicated to Babylon’s gods. They asked the chief official to let them test a diet of vegetables and water for ten days. At the end of ten days they looked healthier than all the others. So the guard took away the royal food, and they ate vegetables from then on.

The Bible says God gave the four young men knowledge and skill in learning. When the king examined them, he found them ten times better than all his magicians and enchanters.""", ("Daniel", 1)),
C("The golden statue", "Nebuchadnezzar built a gold statue about 90 feet tall and ordered everyone to bow to it when the music played.",
"""Some years later, Nebuchadnezzar built a gold statue on the plain of Dura. It was 60 cubits high and 6 cubits wide, about 90 feet by 9 feet. He called all the officials of the empire to a dedication ceremony. A herald announced that when the horn, flute, zither and other instruments sounded, everyone had to fall down and worship the statue. Anyone who refused would be thrown into a blazing furnace at once.

The music played, and the whole crowd bowed. Everyone except three men. Some Babylonian officials who envied them went to the king. “There are certain Jews whom you have set over the affairs of the province of Babylon: Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego. These men pay no attention to you, O king. They do not serve your gods or worship the golden image.” Nebuchadnezzar flew into a rage and had them brought in.""", ("Daniel", 3)),
C("“Even if He does not”", "The king gave them one more chance. They said God could save them, and even if He did not, they would not bow.",
"""The king offered them another chance. “If you are ready when you hear the music, fall down and worship. But if you do not, you will be thrown into the furnace. And who is the god who will deliver you out of my hands?”

Their answer is one of the bravest in the Bible: “O Nebuchadnezzar, we have no need to answer you in this matter. If this be so, our God whom we serve is able to deliver us from the burning fiery furnace, and He will deliver us out of your hand, O king. But if not, be it known to you, O king, that we will not serve your gods or worship the golden image that you have set up.”

They did not know whether God would save them. They said He could, and that they would obey even if He did not. That is real faith: not a guarantee of an easy outcome, but trust in God no matter what.""", ("Daniel", 3)),
C("The fiery furnace", "The furnace was heated seven times hotter. The three men were thrown in, but the king saw four men walking in the fire.",
"""Nebuchadnezzar was furious. He ordered the furnace heated seven times hotter than usual. The strongest soldiers tied up the three men, still wearing their robes, trousers and turbans, and threw them in. The fire was so hot that it killed the soldiers who carried them.

The king stood up in alarm and asked his advisors, “Did we not cast three men bound into the fire?” “True, O king.” “But I see four men unbound, walking in the midst of the fire, and they are not hurt. And the appearance of the fourth is like a son of the gods.”

The king went near the furnace door and called out, “Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, servants of the Most High God, come out!” They walked out, and the officials gathered around. The fire had not harmed their bodies. Not a hair was singed, their clothes were not burned, and there was no smell of smoke on them. The Bible does not say exactly who the fourth man was, but tradition has said it was the Son of God, Jesus, before He was born as a man.""", ("Daniel", 3)),
C("The king’s response", "Nebuchadnezzar praised the God of the three men and promoted them.",
"""Nebuchadnezzar said, “Blessed be the God of Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, who has sent His angel and delivered His servants, who trusted in Him, and set aside the king’s command, and yielded up their bodies rather than serve and worship any god except their own God.” He announced that anyone who spoke against their God would be punished. “For there is no other god who is able to rescue in this way.”

The king then gave the three men higher positions in Babylon. It is the only time in the book of Daniel that we see Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego, but their faith left a mark on a pagan king who ruled the world’s greatest empire.

Their story may have been well known, because the New Testament book of Hebrews recalls people of faith who “quenched the power of fire.”""", ("Hebrews", 11))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Most of us will never face a literal furnace, but we face pressure to bow: to what our workplace expects, what our friends are doing, or what the culture says is normal. These three men show that you can live in a world that doesn’t share your faith and still be loyal to God.

Their words, “but if not,” matter. They trusted God whether or not He rescued them. Faith that depends on a particular outcome is not faith in God. It is faith in the outcome.

And they were not alone. The king saw a fourth person in the fire with them. The Bible promises that God is with His people in hard places: “When you pass through the waters, I will be with you; and through the rivers, they shall not overwhelm you; when you walk through fire you shall not be burned.”""", ("Isaiah", 43))])
